 # Window-Length Encoding
-Also called: RL encoding, Run-Length Encoding
+Also called: RL encoding, Run-Length Encoding (RLE)
 
 ## Overview
-Window-Length Encoding is a data compression technique that scans a source and encodes repeated sequences of a single symbol (runs) by replacing them with a symbol-count pair [S1, unofficial]. In volume rendering, it is applied to intermediate images to reduce network transfer time [S2]. It sets itself apart from standard RLE by using a fixed-length counter based on an expected run length $m$, which simplifies implementation at the cost of efficiency if $m$ is not a power of 2 [S1, unofficial].
+Window-Length Encoding is a data compression technique that scans a source and encodes repeated sequences of a single symbol (runs) by replacing them with a symbol-count pair [S1, unofficial]. In volume rendering, it is applied to intermediate images to reduce network transfer time [S2]. It sets itself apart from variable-length alternatives by using a fixed-length counter based on an expected run length $m$, which simplifies implementation but wastes efficiency if $m$ is not a power of 2 [S1, unofficial].
 
